{
  "comment": "Every instrument here is invented. No name is a real fund or company and no ticker is a listed symbol, as far as web searches, public business registries and the exchanges' symbol lists show. The two coins keep their real tickers because they name an asset class, not a holding. model drives the price engine (demohouse/market.py): beta is the loading on the named factor and idio the annual volatility of the instrument's own noise; a stable price never moves; a bond of type curve, discount or floating prices off the dollar curve at its spread, in percent, until its maturity, a callable one to the worse of its maturity and its call. yield is an annual cash payout as a share of price; a stock with no yield pays the dividend the declarations ledger declares; withholding is the share of a payout withheld at source. A coupon states its rate, or that it floats at a curve tenor plus a spread in percent. A bond that states_terms has a source record stating its coupon and maturity, as the yield pass reads them; any other bond's name states what it states. A money-market fund earns the curve at its tenor less its expenses, in percent. A ladder is a series of discount notes, each issued as the one before matures, which the generator names (issuer, maturity, paper) and numbers (the ISIN prefix and the issue's number). A bond's rating is the one the household records in the declarations ledger; no source states it.",
